<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!--
        Scrollbars, transcribed from the other shell's theme.

            width       10
            track       transparent
            thumb       6 wide, slate-300, radius 3
            hover       slate-400
            arrows      none

        The reason this file exists at all: the platform theme's scrollbar is a wide grey
        trough with a thick handle and two arrow buttons, and it is on screen at all times
        whether or not anyone needs it. It was the first thing on the screen that looked
        like a different product. A ten-pixel trough with a six-pixel handle is almost
        nothing, which is the point.

        One template serves both orientations, as it does in the other shell. Avalonia's own
        template switches on orientation and sizes the handle to the trough; this one does
        not, so a horizontal bar gets the same six-pixel handle - there are no horizontal
        scrollers in this product, and a template that guessed wrong for them would be a
        guess nobody could check.
    -->
    <Style Selector="ScrollBar">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
        <Setter Property="Width" Value="10" />
        <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="10" />
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <ControlTemplate>
                <Panel Background="Transparent">
                    <Track x:Name="PART_Track" IsDirectionReversed="True">
                        <Track.Thumb>
                            <Thumb Classes="slimThumb" />
                        </Track.Thumb>
                    </Track>
                </Panel>
            </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!--
        What this costs, written down: clicking the empty trough no longer pages. The other
        shell has invisible page buttons there, and this engine's track does not accept them
        - set on a track, they are a compile error, not a runtime one. Wheel, dragging the
        handle, the keyboard, the mouse wheel and the scroll-viewer's own keys all work, and
        on a ten-pixel trough the lost gesture is one nobody would have found by accident.
        It goes back the day the track takes them.
    -->

    <Style Selector="Thumb.slimThumb">
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <ControlTemplate>
                <Border Background="{DynamicResource Slate300}" CornerRadius="3"
                        MaxWidth="6" MaxHeight="6"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="Thumb.slimThumb:pointerover /template/ Border">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Slate400}" />
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="Thumb.slimThumb:pressed /template/ Border">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Slate400}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        The trough itself is 1px slate-100 in the other shell, set on the ScrollBar rather
        than on the template, which means it shows through behind the handle. Left
        transparent here on purpose: at ten pixels wide it sits against the page background
        anyway, and a visible stripe down the right-hand edge of every screen is exactly the
        artefact this file was written to remove.

    -->

    <!--
        The list of projects, and only the list of projects.

        The bar is pushed outside the block of cards so the cards can finish at the same
        right-hand edge as the control panel above them. Pushing it out is only half of
        that: a bar pressed flat against the last card looks like part of the card, which
        is what it was asked to stop looking like.

        So this bar gets a wider trough - twenty-two instead of ten - and the handle, which
        sits in the middle of it, ends up eight pixels clear of the cards. The wider trough
        is invisible: it is transparent, and the window's own margin is wide enough to hold
        it.

        Only this scroller. The books on the other screens keep the ten-pixel trough the
        other shell has, because those screens were not part of this change and a change
        nobody asked for is still a change.
    -->
    <Style Selector="ScrollViewer.projectsScroll /template/ ScrollBar">
        <Setter Property="Width" Value="22" />
        <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="22" />
    </Style>
</Styles>
